CAT = "caautoOcc"; SEG = "OCC"
# RSOS 2023 MWA A: Performs common occupational skills (A-1 safety, A-2 tools, equipment and documentation)
Q = [
("Before raising a vehicle on a two-post hoist, where should the lift arms be placed?",
 "On the maker's lifting points", ["Under the plastic rocker panels", "Under the front crossmember", "Wherever the pads sit level"],
 "Service information shows the lifting points that can carry the vehicle's weight and keep it balanced. Trim, panels and suspension parts can crush or let the vehicle slide off the pads."),
("After lifting a vehicle with a floor jack, what must you do before working underneath?",
 "Set it on rated jack stands", ["Leave the jack under load", "Pump the jack to full height", "Chock only the front wheels"],
 "A floor jack can leak down or slip, so it is for lifting only. Rated stands at the correct support points hold the load before anyone goes under."),
("Which fire extinguisher class is meant for burning gasoline or oil?",
 "Class B", ["Class A", "Class C", "Class D"],
 "Class B covers flammable liquids such as fuels, oils and solvents. Class A is ordinary combustibles, C is energized electrical equipment and D is combustible metals."),
("Where do you find a product's hazards, PPE and first aid measures under WHMIS?",
 "The safety data sheet", ["The shop work order", "The supplier's invoice", "The service bulletin"],
 "Under WHMIS every hazardous product comes with a 16-section safety data sheet (SDS) listing hazards, protective equipment, first aid and handling. The supplier label gives a summary only."),
("How should brake dust be cleaned from drum brake parts?",
 "Wet wipe or HEPA vacuum", ["Blow off with compressed air", "Wipe with a dry shop rag", "Sweep with a dry brush"],
 "Brake dust is a respiratory hazard and may contain asbestos on older parts. Wet cleaning or a HEPA vacuum keeps it out of the air; blowing or dry brushing spreads it."),
("What is the purpose of lockout before servicing shop equipment such as a tire changer?",
 "To prevent an unexpected start", ["To record the hours of use", "To keep tools from being taken", "To speed up the repair"],
 "Lockout isolates the energy source and keeps it isolated with a personal lock, so nobody can start the machine while you are in it. Procedures vary by province but follow this principle."),
("When should a click-type torque wrench be set to its lowest setting?",
 "When it is put away", ["Before every single use", "Only when calibrating it", "When tightening small bolts"],
 "Storing it at a high setting keeps the spring compressed and lets calibration drift. Backing it off to the lowest marked setting, not below it, protects accuracy."),
("A head bolt is listed as torque-to-yield. What does that normally mean for reuse?",
 "Replace it with a new bolt", ["Reuse it if threads look good", "Reuse it with thread locker", "Reuse it once, then replace"],
 "A torque-to-yield bolt is stretched into its yield range on installation and will not clamp correctly a second time. Most makers require new bolts and an angle-torque procedure."),
("A metric bolt head is stamped 10.9. What does the 10 tell you?",
 "Its tensile strength class", ["Its thread pitch in mm", "Its length in centimetres", "Its head size in mm"],
 "The first number is about one hundredth of the minimum tensile strength in MPa, so 10.9 is roughly 1000 MPa. The second number is the yield-to-tensile ratio, here 90%."),
("Which VIN character shows a vehicle's model year?",
 "The 10th character", ["The 1st character", "The 8th character", "The 17th character"],
 "The 17-character VIN puts the model year in position 10. Position 1 is the country of manufacture and position 8 is often the engine code."),
("Which tool measures crankshaft journal diameter most accurately?",
 "An outside micrometer", ["A digital dial caliper", "A feeler gauge set", "A dial bore gauge"],
 "An outside micrometer reads external diameters to 0.01 mm or finer. A dial bore gauge measures inside diameters such as cylinder bores."),
("What does a dial indicator measure when mounted against a brake rotor face?",
 "Lateral runout", ["Rotor thickness", "Rotor hardness", "Surface finish"],
 "With the rotor turning, the indicator shows side-to-side wobble, called lateral runout. Thickness variation is measured with a micrometer at several points."),
("What is Plastigage used to check?",
 "Bearing oil clearance", ["Cylinder bore taper", "Valve spring height", "Piston ring end gap"],
 "The plastic thread is crushed between the bearing and journal, and its width against the scale gives the oil clearance. Ring end gap is checked with a feeler gauge."),
("Where should you first look for a known fix to a common customer concern?",
 "Technical service bulletins", ["The vehicle owner's manual", "The parts supplier catalogue", "Online owner forum posts"],
 "Manufacturers issue technical service bulletins (TSBs) for known problems, often with revised parts or updated software. Checking them early can save hours of diagnosis."),
("A meter is rated CAT III 1000 V. What does the rating describe?",
 "Its overvoltage protection", ["Its accuracy on DC volts", "Its battery life in hours", "Its maximum current range"],
 "The category rating says how well the meter and leads withstand transient spikes at a given working voltage. High-voltage hybrid and EV work calls for at least CAT III 1000 V."),
("Before using a refrigerant recovery machine on a vehicle, what should you check first?",
 "The refrigerant's identity", ["The compressor clutch gap", "The cabin air filter", "The blower motor speed"],
 "A refrigerant identifier confirms the type and purity so you do not contaminate the recovery machine or its stock. Mixed or unknown refrigerant goes into a separate container."),
]
